  "worked_solution": "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=4(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4{u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=28{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =28{u}^{6}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =28(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)^{6}(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =-28{cos}^{6}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
